const r=11,a="Migratory Bird Diversity in Wetlands of the Eastern Zone of Uttar Pradesh (India)",e="Mudit Prakash and Asha Rani",i=[140,150],t=["Department of Zoology,","Tilak Dhari P.G College, Jaunpur,","Affiliated to V.B.S. Purvanchal University, Jaunpur,","*Corresponding author","Dr. Asha Rani","Tilak Dhari P.G. College, Jaunpur,","Affiliated to V.B.S Purvanchal University, Jaunpur,","*E-mail - anshuaucky@gmail.com"],n=[{t:"h",x:"Abstract:"},{t:"p",x:"Eastern Uttar Pradesh wetlands sustain a wide variety of migratory birds during their periodic migrations, making them an essential component of the Central Asian Flyway (Boere and Stroud, 2006; Kirby et al., 2008). Using standard field techniques such as point counts and direct observations, this study investigates the diversity, distribution, and ecological significance of migratory avifauna in the floodplain wetlands of districts including Jaunpur, Varanasi, Gorakhpur, Mirzapur, and Azamgarh. It shows that these wetlands are home to a variety of waterfowl, waders, and passerine migrants, especially during the winter season (November– February) (Ali, 2002; Grimmett et al., 2011; Newton, 2008). The Northern Pintail (Anas acuta), Bar-headed Goose (Anser indicus), and several heron, egret, and shorebird species are among the important species noted. The results show that seasonal hydrological fluctuations, food availability, and habitat diversity significantly affect bird diversity trends. However, these migratory populations are seriously threatened by increasing anthropogenic pressures, including pollution, agricultural expansion, wetland degradation, and climate change (Davidson, 2014; IPCC, 2022; Sala et al., 2000). To preserve these ecologically significant ecosystems and their avifaunal diversity, the study emphasizes the critical need for sustainable wetland management, conservation awareness, and long-term monitoring programs (IUCN, 2023; BirdLife International, 2022). Keywords: Migratory birds, Wetlands; Eastern Uttar Pradesh, Avian diversity, Central Asian Flyway, Waterfowl, Biodiversity conservation, Seasonal migration, Habitat ecology, Wetland management."},{t:"h",x:"Introduction"},{t:"p",x:"A vital component of avifaunal variety, particularly migratory birds, wetlands are among the planet's most productive ecosystems (Keddy, 2010; Junk et al., 2013). Many migratory bird species that arrive from Siberia, Central Asia, and Europe use Eastern Uttar Pradesh (UP), situated within the Central Asian Flyway, as a major wintering and stopover site (Boere and Stroud, 2006; CMS, 2020)."},{t:"p",x:"In response to seasonal variations, food availability, and breeding needs, migratory birds migrate thousands of kilometers every year (Newton, 2008; Alerstam et al., 2003). For feeding, breeding, and resting, the"},{t:"h",x:"CHAPTER 11"},{t:"h",x:"MIGRATORY BIRD DIVERSITY IN WETLANDS OF THE EASTERN ZONE OF UTTAR PRADESH (INDIA)"},{t:"p",x:"wetlands of eastern Uttar Pradesh, including oxbow lakes, marshes, floodplains, and riverine systems, offer perfect environments (Gosselink, 2015; Ramsar Convention Secretariat, 2016). High biodiversity and minimal ecological services like nitrogen cycling, flood management, and water purification are supported by the habitat (Junk et al., 2013; Kingford et al., 2016)."},{t:"h",x:"Study Area: Wetlands in Eastern Uttar Pradesh"},{t:"p",x:"Districts in Eastern Uttar Pradesh, including Jaunpur, Mirzapur, Varanasi, Gorakhpur, Azamgarh, and Gonda, are distinguished by: (ZSI, 2019; BNHS, 2020). The Ganga, Gomti, Varuna, and Sai rivers, among others, have floodplain wetlands. Seasonal ponds and marshes Wetlands' adjacent agricultural landscapes Research reveals that 119 bird species from 52 groups were found in Gonda district's unprotected wetlands, showing a significant potential for biodiversity. Ulman and Sinagh (2026)."},{t:"h",x:"Major Migratory Bird Species"},{t:"h",x:"Common migratory birds observed in eastern UP wetlands include:"},{t:"h",x:"Waterfowl :"},{t:"p",x:"Bar-headed Goose (Anser indicus) – High-altitude Central Asia; breeds May–June (~5–15°C); migrates Tibet/Central Asia → Himalaya cross karke India (Central Asian Flyway) (Ali & Ripley, 1987). Greylag Goose (Anser anser) – Temperate Europe & Asia; breeds Apr–Jun (~10–20°C); migrates Europe/Central Asia → South Asia (BirdLife International, 2020)."},{t:"p",x:"Northern Pintail (Anas acuta) – Temperate & Arctic regions; breeds May–Jul (~5–15°C); migrates Eurasia → India & Africa via Central Asian Flyway (Grimmett et al., 2011)."},{t:"p",x:"White fronted Geese (Anser albifrons) – Arctic tundra; breeds May–Jun (~0–10°C); migrates Arctic → Central Asia → India (wintering) (BirdLife International, 2020)."},{t:"c",x:"Figure 1. White fronted Geese (Sources: eBird, 2023)"},{t:"h",x:"Waders :"},{t:"p",x:"Black-tailed Godwit (Limosa limosa) Temperate, breeds April- June (~10–20°C); migrates Central Asia/Europe - India via Central Asian Flyway (BirdLife International, 2020)."},{t:"p",x:"Common Sandpiper (Actitis hypoleucos) – Temperate Eurasia; breeds May–Jul (~8–18°C); migrates to South Asia & Africa via Central Asian Flyway (Grimmett et al., 2011)."},{t:"p",x:"Grey-headed Swamphen (Porphyrio poliocephalus) – Tropical wetlands; breeds Jun–Sep (~25–35°C); mostly resident with local movement (Ali and Ripley, 198)"},{t:"c",x:"Figure 2. Grey headed Swampen (Sources: eBird, 2023)"},{t:"h",x:"Herons and Egrets :"},{t:"p",x:"Grey Heron (Ardea cinerea)– Temperate/tropical wetlands; breeds Feb–Jun (~15–25°C); migrates Europe/Asia → South Asia (Kushlan and Hancock, 2005)."},{t:"p",x:"Purple Heron (Ardea purpurea) – Warm wetlands; breeds May–Jul (~20–30°C); migrates Europe → Africa & South Asia (BirdLife International, 2020)."},{t:"p",x:"Little Egret (Egretta garzetta) – Tropical/subtropical wetlands; breeds Apr–Aug (~20–30°C); partial migrant (Grimmett et al., 2011)."},{t:"c",x:"Figure 3. Sources: eBird, 2023"},{t:"h",x:"Others"},{t:"p",x:"Amur Falcon (Falco amurensis) – Temperate East Asia; breeds May–Jun (~15–25°C); migrates Asia → India → Africa (Symes, 2012)."},{t:"p",x:"Rosy Starling (Pastor roseus) – Steppe regions; breeds May–Jun (~20–35°C); migrates Central Asia → India (Ali and Ripley, 1987)."},{t:"p",x:"Common Teal (Rallus aquaticus) – Temperate wetlands; breeds Apr–Jul"},{t:"p",x:"(~10–20°C); migrates Europe → South Asia (Taylor and van Perlo, 1998)."},{t:"h",x:"Representative Migratory Birds"},{t:"c",x:"Figure 4. Indian pitta; (Image sources: eBird, 2023)"},{t:"h",x:"Painted Stork & Wetland Birds"},{t:"p",x:"(Images are representative of species commonly found in North Indian wetlands.)"},{t:"l",x:["Diversity Trends","In eastern UP wetlands, bird diversity is dependent upon:","The Hydrology Structure of Vegetation","Disturbance by humans","Food supplies are accessible (Kumar et al., 2015).","1. Seasonal Differences"]},{t:"p",x:"Diversity peaks from November to February. October to November is when winter migrants arrive (Grimmett et al., 2011; BirdLIfe International, 2023)."},{t:"h",x:"2. Kind of Habitat"},{t:"p",x:"Diving ducks in deep swamps Waders in shallow wetlands (Kushwaha et al., 2018)."},{t:"h",x:"3. Food Accessibility"},{t:"p",x:"Aquatic vegetation provides food for herbivorous birds. Piscivorous animals are drawn to fish populations (Dutta and Maity, 2017)."},{t:"l",x:["METHODOLOGY (For Field Research)","Typical research techniques consist of:","> Point count technique. (sampling radius of 20 m)","> Transect surveys in lines.","> Observing directly via binoculars.","> Photographic records (Bibby et al., 2000)."]},{t:"c",x:"Figure 5. Major threat faced by migratory birds (Sources: Adopted from BirdLife International, 2023)"},{t:"h",x:"Threats to Migratory Birds"},{t:"c",x:"Figure 6. Migratory birds face multiple threats across spatial scale, significantly affecting their population dynamics and survival rates. (IUCN, 2022)"},{t:"l",x:["1 Human-Related Dangers","> Encroachment into wetlands. (Finlayson et al., 2018)","> growth of agriculture. (Foley et al., 20110).","> Eutrophication and pollution. (Smith et al., 1999)."]},{t:"p",x:"Human actions that adversely affect migrating birds and wetlands are known as anthropogenic hazards. These pressures are rising rapidly in wetlands across eastern Uttar Pradesh, including marshes, floodplains, and oxbow lakes (Kumar et al., 2020; Ramsar Convention, 2016)."},{t:"h",x:"1.1. Destruction of Habitats"},{t:"p",x:"For infrastructure, agriculture, and urban growth, wetlands are drained (MEA, 2005; Davidson, 2014). Loss of food and breeding habitats (Mitsch and Gosselink, 2007; Weller, 1999)"},{t:"h",x:"1.2. Contamination"},{t:"p",x:"Pesticides, fertilizers, sewage, and industrial waste (Carpenter et al., 1998; Schiesari et al., 2019) lower bird food; kill fish and plankton; and cause water toxicity (Fleeger et al., 2003; Islam and Tanaka, 2004)."},{t:"h",x:"1.3. Increasing Agricultural Production"},{t:"p",x:"Overuse of chemical pesticides and fertilizers (Vitousek et al., 1997; Tilman et al., 2002) causes bird poisoning and eutrophication, or algal bloom (Smith and Schindler, 2009)."},{t:"h",x:"1.4. Resource exploitation and overfishing"},{t:"p",x:"Fish population reduction leads to a shortage of food for birds (Pauly et al., 2002; Jackson et al., 2001)."},{t:"h",x:"1.5. Poaching & Hunting"},{t:"p",x:"Hunting migrating birds illegally for trade or food (Brochet et al., 2016; CMS, 2019). Migration patterns are disturbed (BirdLife International, 2021)."},{t:"h",x:"1.6. Human-caused climate change"},{t:"p",x:"Modifies water availability and rainfall patterns (IPCC, 2021). impacts wetland conditions and the time of migration (Both et al., 2006; Walther et al., 2002)."},{t:"h",x:"1.7. Invasion"},{t:"p",x:"Wetlands and human settlements (McKinney, 2002; Grimm et al., 2008). Noise, tourists, and animal grazing cause disturbances (Marzluff, 2001)."},{t:"l",x:["Diagram: Anthropogenic Threat Cycle :-","Human Activities (urbanization, farming, industry)","Habitat Degradation (wetland loss & pollution)","Food Chain Disruption (loss of fish, plankton)","Decline in Bird Diversity","Ecological Imbalance","Reduced Ecosystem Services","Further Human Impact","Cause–Effect Flow Diagram :-","Pollution / Chemicals","Water Contamination","Aquatic Life Decline","Reduced Food Availability","Migratory Bird Decline"]},{t:"h",x:"Climate Change"},{t:"h",x:"Altered migration patterns"},{t:"h",x:"Reduced water availability"},{t:"c",x:"Figure 7. Sources: Ramsar,2021; MoEFCC (2023), Climate change has cascading impacts on migratory bird populations by affecting breeding ground and food resources (Walther et al., 2002; Parmesan, 2006)."},{t:"h",x:"3. Disturbance and Hunting"},{t:"h",x:"Unlawful poaching Interference by humans"},{t:"p",x:"Human disturbance reduces reproductive success by interfering with eating and nesting behavior (Gill et al., 2001)"},{t:"h",x:"4. Conservation Strategies"},{t:"p",x:"International wetland preservation through Ramsar site designation (Ramsar Convention Secretariat, 2021). Programs for raising community awareness (IUCN, 20210). Restoration of wetlands (Zedler, 2000; Mitsch and Gosselink, 2007)."},{t:"p",x:"Programs for monitoring and counting birds (BirdLife International, 2021; MoEFCC, 2023). Every year, hundreds of migratory birds visit wetlands like bird sanctuaries in UP, underscoring their significance for conservation. C"},{t:"c",x:"Figure 8. Wetlands like bird sanctuaries in UP host thousands of migratory birds annually, highlighting their conservation importance (Singh and Kumar, 2022; Kumar et al., 2025)"},{t:"h",x:"Conclusion"},{t:"p",x:"The floodplain wetlands of eastern Uttar Pradesh, which are dispersed throughout districts like Varanasi, Gorakhpur, Mirzapur, Azamgarh, Gonda, and particularly Jaunpur, are extremely important ecological zones in the Central Asian Flyway. For migratory birds, these wetlands—which include village ponds, seasonal marshes, oxbow lakes, and riverine floodplains—provide crucial wintering and stopping locations. Many bird species come to this area from Europe, Central Asia, and Siberia each year."},{t:"p",x:"Rich bird diversity may be found in the area, with species like the Grey-headed Swamphen (Porphyrio poliocephalus), Northern Pintail (Anas acuta), and Bar-headed Goose (Anser indicus). Different bird species require different types of habitat; for example, waders require shallow muddy areas, whereas diving ducks prefer deep water bodies. The Gomti and Sai river basins contain the Jaunpur district, which is a significant hub for migrating birds. This region's seasonal agricultural water bodies and rural wetlands offer a wealth of food resources, including fish, aquatic plants, and macroinvertebrates, providing the perfect environment for feeding and resting."},{t:"p",x:"Bird migration in this area is significantly influenced by the climate. Flooding occurs during the monsoon season, and migratory birds benefit greatly from the calm, steady winter months of November through February. However, these migration cycles are becoming more and more impacted by climate change, erratic rainfall patterns, and rising temperatures. Furthermore, these wetlands are seriously threatened by anthropogenic pressures including illegal hunting, pollution, agricultural development, and chemical runoff that results in an excess of nutrients in other water bodies."},{t:"h",x:"References :-"},{t:"p",x:"Ali, S. (2002). The Book of Indian Birds (13th revised ed.). Mumbai: Bombay Natural History Society and Oxford University Press."},{t:"p",x:"Annapoorneshwari, H. (2026). Wetland avifauna diversity review. Uttar Pradesh Journal of Zoology. BirdLife International. (2022). State of the World’s Birds: Taking the pulse of the planet. Cambridge, UK: BirdLife International."},{t:"p",x:"BirdLife International. (2023). Migratory bird data and flyway conservation protocols. Cambridge, UK: BirdLife International."},{t:"p",x:"Bombay Natural History Society [BNHS]. (2021). Bird migration studies in India: Annual research and banding report. Mumbai: BNHS India."},{t:"p",x:"Boere and Stroud, 2006; Kirby et al., ( 2008). essential component of the Central Asian Flyway. Bora, S. S., et al. (2024). Migratory bird diversity in wetlands: Habitat preference and seasonal variations. Uttar Pradesh Journal of Zoology."},{t:"p",x:"Chatterjee, S. (2017). Migratory routes of waterfowl across the Central Asian Flyway. Indian Forester. Das, S. (2018). Wetland biodiversity and threats in North India. International Journal of Ecology, 2018. FAO. (2021). Wetland ecosystem services and their valuation in agricultural landscapes. Rome: Food and Agriculture Organization of the United Nations."},{t:"p",x:"Gopal, B. (2013). Wetlands and biodiversity: The Indian scenario. Environmental Reviews. Grimmett, R., Inskipp, C., & Inskipp, T. (2011). Birds of the Indian Subcontinent (2nd ed.). London: Christopher Helm & Oxford University Press."},{t:"p",x:"Gupta, R. (2020). Avian ecology and seasonal variations in Uttar Pradesh. Current Science. Islam, M. Z. (2010). Waterbird ecology and habitat selection. Journal of Bombay Natural History Society. IUCN. (2023). The IUCN Red List of Threatened Species. Version 2023-1."},{t:"p",x:"Jha, K. (2019). Ecology of seasonal wetlands in the Indo-Gangetic plains. Wetlands Ecology and Management. Joshi, O., Singh, N., & Sathiyaselvam, P. (2024). Waterbird diversity of Saman Wetland Complex. Journal of Threatened Taxa."},{t:"p",x:"Keddy, P. A. (2010). Wetland Ecology: Principles and Conservation (2nd ed.). Cambridge: Cambridge University Press."},{t:"p",x:"Khan, A. (2016). Bird conservation through community participation. Journal Biodiversity. Kumar, A. (2024). Pollution risks on waterbirds in the floodplain wetlands of eastern Uttar Pradesh. Journal of Environmental Biology."},{t:"p",x:"Kumar, P. (2018). Wetland birds of North India: An ecological perspective. Zoological Research. Kumar, S. (2023). Biodiversity conservation in rural wetlands. Journal of Applied Ecology. Kumari, T., et al. (2025). Migratory birds in Population dynamics and conservation challenges. Asian Journal of Conservation Biology."},{t:"p",x:"Mishra, A. (2021). Wetland ecosystems and their conservation. Indian Journal of Forestry. Mitsch, W. J., and Gosselink, J. G. (2007). Wetlands (4th ed.). Hoboken, New Jersey: John Wiley and Sons. MoEFCC India. (2023). National Action Plan for Migratory Birds and Wetlands along the Central Asian Flyway (2023–2027). New Delhi: Ministry of Environment, Forest and Climate Change. Pandey, N. (2023). Bird habitats in the floodplain wetlands of Eastern UP. Avian Conservation and Ecology. Prasad, S. N. (2002). Conservation of wetlands of India: A national priority. Ambio: A Journal of the Human Environment."},{t:"p",x:"Ramsar Convention Secretariat. (2021). Wetland conservation guidelines and global assessment. Gland, Switzerland: Ramsar Convention Secretariat."},{t:"p",x:"Roy, P. (2020). Bird migration patterns and climate change impacts. Global Ecology and Conservation, 2024. Scott, D. A. (1989). A directory of Asian wetlands. Gland, Switzerland: IUCN."},{t:"p",x:"Sharma, S. (2019). Bird diversity analysis: Field methods and metrics. Journal of Bioscience. Singh, P., et al. (2025). Migratory birds of Uttar Pradesh: A comprehensive distribution analysis. Journal of Science Innovations."},{t:"p",x:"Singh, R. (2021). Wetland management in the Gangetic plains. Water Resources Management. Singh, V. (2022). Waterbird studies in dynamic river basins. Hydrobiologia."},{t:"p",x:"Tiwari, P. (2024). Wetland conservation and community-led restoration. Environmental Management. Turner, R. K. (2018). Wetland valuation and ecological economics. Ecological Economics. Ulman, Y., and Singh, M. (2026). Bird diversity in wetlands of Gonda district, Eastern Uttar Pradesh. Journal of Applied Natural Science."},{t:"p",x:"Verma, D. (2022). Migratory birds in India: Long-term trends. Journal of Ornithology. Verma, M. (2015). Economic valuation of wetlands in India. Indian Journal of Agricultural Economics. Wetlands International. (2022). Asian Waterbird Census: Status and trends of waterbird populations. Wageningen, Netherlands."},{t:"p",x:"Wildlife Institute of India [WII]. (2022). Wetland biodiversity report and management framework for North India. Dehradun."},{t:"p",x:"Yadav, D. (2022). Avifaunal diversity of seasonal pools in Jaunpur. Indian Journal of Animal Sciences. Zoological Survey of India [ZSI]. (2020). Avifauna of India: Diversity, distribution and conservation status."}],o={n:11,title:a,authors:e,pages:i,affiliations:t,blocks:n};export{t as affiliations,e as authors,n as blocks,o as default,r as n,i as pages,a as title};
